    <item>
      <title>CSS Introduction | Advanced CSS | Image Sprite</title>
      <link>https://www.devkuma.com/en/docs/css/image-sprite/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 10 Jul 2017 23:13:18 +0900</pubDate>
      <author>kc@example.com (kc kim)</author>
      <guid>https://www.devkuma.com/en/docs/css/image-sprite/</guid>
      <description>
        
        
        &lt;h3 id=&#34;css-image-sprite&#34;&gt;CSS Image Sprite&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An image sprite is an image that combines several images into a single image for easier management.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When images are used on a web page, the browser requests each image from the server in order to download it.&lt;br&gt;
If many images are used, the browser must send as many requests as there are images, which increases the page loading time.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Using an image sprite can reduce the number of server requests needed to download images to just a few.&lt;br&gt;
On platforms with limited resources, such as mobile environments, this helps shorten web page loading time.&lt;br&gt;
It is also convenient because you only need to manage a small number of sprite image files instead of many individual image files.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34;&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;style&amp;gt;
    .up, .down, .right, .left { background: url(&amp;#34;/examples/images/img_image_sprites.png&amp;#34;) no-repeat; }
    .up { width: 21px; height: 20px; background-position: 0 0; }
    .down { width: 21px; height: 20px; background-position: -21px 0; }
    .right { width: 22px; height: 20px; background-position: -42px 0; }
    .left { width: 22px; height: 20px; background-position: -65px 0; }
&amp;lt;/style&amp;gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
      </description>
      
      <category>CSS</category>
      
      <category>WEB</category>
      
    </item>
    
